><div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Critical-Section Problem
 =E2=80=A2 Consider a system consisting of n processes {P0, P1,..., Pn=E2=
=88=921}.=20
=E2=80=A2 A Critical Section is a section of code common to cooperating pro=
cesses, where
 the process may be accessing and updating shared data (like variables, fil=
es,
 databases).
 =E2=80=A2 The Critical Section Problem arises when multiple processes acce=
ss and update
 share resources at the same time.
 =E2=80=A2 The important feature of the system is that, when one process is=
 executing in its
 critical section, no other process is allowed to execute in its critical s=
ection.=20
=E2=80=A2 That is, no two processes are executing in their critical section=
s at the same time.=20
=E2=80=A2 The critical-section problem is to design a protocol that the pro=
cesses can be
 allowed to  share  data without causing  data inconsistencies.=20
Dr. Venkata Rami Reddy Ch , Sr. Assistant Professor, SCOPE
                                                7
Critical-Section Problem
 Critical Section=20
=E2=80=A2The part of a process where shared variables or
 resources are accessed/modified.
 Entry Section=20
=E2=80=A2The entry section is the part of code where a process
 requests permission to enter its critical section
 Exit Section=20
=E2=80=A2The exit section is the part of the code where a
 process leaves its critical section and signals other
 processes that they can enter.
 Remainder Section
 =E2=80=A2 The rest of the process outside the critical section,
 which does not use shared resources..
 General structure of process Pi=20
Dr. Venkata Rami Reddy Ch , Sr. Assistant Professor, SCOPE
                                                8
Requirements of a CSP Solution
 =E2=80=A2 Any solution to the Critical-Section Problem must satisfy these =
three conditions:
 Mutual Exclusion
 =E2=80=A2 Only one process at a time can be executing in their critical se=
ction.
 Progress
 =E2=80=A2 If no process is executing in its critical section and some proc=
esses wish
 to enter their critical sections, then only those processes that are not
 executing in their remainder sections can participate in deciding which
 will enter its critical section next, and this selection cannot be
 postponed indefinitely
 Bounded waiting=20
=E2=80=A2 There exists a limit on the number of times that other processes =
are
 allowed to enter their critical sections after a process has made a reques=
t to
 enter its critical section and before that request is granted.</div></div>=
