=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Hardware Support for Synchronization
 =E2=80=A2Software-based solutions are not guaranteed to work on modern com=
puter
 architectures.
 =E2=80=A2Many modern computer systems provide special hardware instruction=
s such as
 test_and_set() , compare_and_swap() hardware instructions to solve the cri=
tical
section problem in a relatively simple manner.
test_and_set()
 How it Works
 The instruction does two things atomically:
 =E2=80=A2 Reads the current value of a memory location (say a lock variabl=
e).
 =E2=80=A2 Sets that memory location to 1 (locked).
 Then it returns the old value.
Mutual-exclusion implementation with test_and_set()
 The structure of process Pi:
 =E2=80=A2 variable lock, initialized to false=20
for first process
 lock=3Dfalse
Mutual-exclusion implementation with test_and_set()
 Why It Ensures Mutual Exclusion
 If two processes call test_and_set(&amp;lock) at the same time:
 =E2=80=A2 Only one will see the old value false (0) and set it to true (1)=
.
 =E2=80=A2 The other(s) will see true and will spin in the loop.
 So only one process enters the critical section at a time.</div></div></di=
