<div class=3D"whitespace-pre-wrap">Semaphores
 wait():=20
=E2=80=A2 It decrements the semaphore value by 1.
 Definition:
 wait(S) {
 while (S &lt;=3D 0)
 ; // busy wait
 S--;
 }
 signal():=20
it increments the semaphore value by 1.
 Definition :
 signal(S) {
 S++;
 }
Types of Semaphores
 Semaphores are mainly of two types:
 1. Binary semaphore
 2. Counting semaphore
 Binary semaphore:
 =E2=80=A2 It is a semaphore whose value is either 0 or 1.
 =E2=80=A2 Binary  semaphore can be used to control access to a single reso=
urce.
 =E2=80=A2 It is also called mutex locks to provide mutual exclusion.
 Counting semaphore:
 =E2=80=A2 It is a semaphore whose value is between 0 or positive number(no=
 of
 resources available)
 =E2=80=A2 Counting semaphores can be used to control access to a given res=
ource=20
consisting of a finite number of instances.
Semaphore Usage
 =E2=80=A2 The semaphore is initialized to the number of resources availabl=
e.=20
=E2=80=A2 Each process that wishes to use a resource performs a wait() oper=
ation on
 the semaphore.
 =E2=80=A2 When a process releases a resource, it performs a signal() opera=
tion.
 =E2=80=A2 When the count for the semaphore goes to 0, all resources are be=
ing used.
 =E2=80=A2 After that, processes that wish to use a resource will block unt=
il the count
 becomes greater than zero.
Semaphore Implementation
 Mutual-exclusion implementation with semaphore:
 mutex=3D1
 do {
 wait (mutex) ;
 II critical section
 signal(mutex);
 II remainder section
 } while (TRUE);
 wait(S) {
 while (S &lt;=3D 0)
 ; =EE=8D=83=EE=9B=AE busy wait
 S--;
 }
 =E2=80=A2 The main disadvantage of the semaphore definition given here is =
that it requires
 busy waiting.
 =E2=80=A2 While a process is in its critical section, any other process th=
at tries to enter its
 critical section must loop continuously in the wait operation.=20
=E2=80=A2 Busy waiting wastes CPU cycles.
Semaphore Implementation without busy waiting
 =E2=80=A2 To overcome the need for busy waiting, we can modify the definit=
ion of the
 wait() and signal() semaphore operations.=20
=E2=80=A2 When a process executes the wait () operation and finds that the =
semaphore
 value is not positive, it must wait.=20
=E2=80=A2 However, rather than engaging in busy waiting, the process can bl=
ock itself.
 =E2=80=A2 The block operation places a process into a waiting queue, and t=
he state of
 the process is switched to the waiting state.
 =E2=80=A2 A process that is blocked, should be restarted when some other p=
rocess
 executes a signal() operation.=20
=E2=80=A2 The process is restarted by a wakeup () operation, which changes =
the process
 from the waiting state to the ready state.
Semaphore Implementation without busy waiting
 =E2=80=A2 To implement semaphores under this definition, we define a semap=
hore as
 a "C' struct:
 typedef struct {
 int value;
 struct process *list;
 } semaphore;
 =E2=80=A2 Each semaphore has an integer value and a list.=20
=E2=80=A2 When a process must wait on a semaphore, it is added to the list.
Semaphore Implementation without busy waiting
 =E2=80=A2 In the modified wait(), we check S-&gt;value &lt; 0.
 =E2=80=A2If it=E2=80=99s negative, it means resources are unavailable and=
=20
the current process must wait.
 =E2=80=A2S-&gt;value =3D -2 means 2 processes are in the waiting=20
queue.
