e Readers-Writers Problem
 =E2=80=A2 A database is to be shared among several concurrent processes.
 =E2=80=A2 Readers =E2=80=93 only read the data from data base; they do not=
 perform any updates
 =E2=80=A2 Writers   =E2=80=93 Update the data in database, requiring exclu=
sive access.
 Constraints:
 =E2=80=A2 Writers require exclusive access =E2=86=92 no other readers or w=
riters can access while
 writing.
 =E2=80=A2 Any number of readers may read the data simultaneously.
 =E2=80=A2 If a write is updating the data no reader may read it.
The Readers-Writers Problem
 =E2=80=A2 In the solution to the readers=E2=80=93writers problem, the read=
er processes share the following
 data structures:
 =E2=80=A2 semaphore rw_mutex =3D 1;=20
=E2=80=A2 semaphore mutex =3D 1;=20
=E2=80=A2 int read_count =3D 0;=20
=E2=80=A2 The mutex semaphore is used to ensure mutual exclusion when the v=
ariable read_count
 is updated.=20
=E2=80=A2 The read_count variable keeps track of how many processes are cur=
rently reading the
 object.
 =E2=80=A2 The semaphore rw_mutex functions as a mutual exclusion semaphore=
 for the writers.=20
=E2=80=A2 It is also used by the first or last reader that enters or exits =
the critical section.=20
=E2=80=A2 It is not used by readers who enter or exit while other readers a=
re in their critical
 sections.
The Readers-Writers Problem
 The structure of a writer process
 while (true) {
 wait(rw_mutex);=20
...
 /* writing is performed */=20
...=20
signal(rw_mutex);=20
     }
The Readers-Writers Problem
 The structure of a reader process
 while (true){
 wait(mutex);
 read_count++;
 if (read_count =3D=3D 1)   =20
     wait(rw_mutex);=20
     signal(mutex);=20
...
 /* first reader */=20
/* reading is performed */=20
...=20
wait(mutex);
 read_count--;
 if (read_count =3D=3D 0)  =20
signal(rw_mutex);=20
signal(mutex);=20
/* last reader */
       }


