 =E2=80=A2 Five philosophers sit around a circular table.
 =E2=80=A2 Each philosopher has a chair and alternates between two activiti=
es: thinking and
 eating.
 =E2=80=A2 A bowl of rice is placed at the center of the table.
 =E2=80=A2 Between each pair of philosophers is a single chopstick, so ther=
e are five chopsticks
 in total.
 =E2=80=A2 To eat, a philosopher must acquire both the left and right
 chopsticks.=20
=E2=80=A2 After eating, they put down both chopsticks and return to
 thinking.
 =E2=80=A2 A philosopher may pick up only one chopstick at a time.
 =E2=80=A2 Obviously, she cannot pickup a chopstick that is already in
 the hand of a neighbor.
The Dining-Philosophers Problem
 =E2=80=A2 One simple solution is to represent each chopstick with a semaph=
ore.
 =E2=80=A2 A philosopher tries to grab a chopstick by executing a wait() op=
eration on that
 semaphore.
 =E2=80=A2 She releases her chopsticks by executing the signal() operation =
on the appropriate
 semaphores
 =E2=80=A2 Thus, the shared data are
 =E2=80=A2 semaphore chopstick[5]; =20
=E2=80=A2 where all the elements of chopstick are initialized to 1.
The Dining-Philosophers Problem
 The structure of Philosopher i :
 semaphore chopstick[5] =3D {1, 1, 1, 1, 1};
 while (true){=20
    wait(chopstick[i] );=20
 wait(chopStick[ (i + 1) % 5] );=20
/* eat for awhile */
 signal (chopstick[i] );
 // pick left
 // pick right
 // put left
 signal (chopstick[ (i + 1) % 5] ); // put right
 /* think for awhile */
 }
The Dining-Philosophers Problem
 What is the problem with this algorithm?
 =E2=80=A2 Although this solution guarantees that no two neighbors are eati=
ng simultaneously,
 it  could create a deadlock.=20
=E2=80=A2 Suppose that all five philosophers become hungry at the same time=
 and each grabs
 her left chopstick.
 =E2=80=A2 All the elements of chopstick will now be equal to 0.=20
=E2=80=A2 When each philosopher tries to grab her right chopstick, she will=
 be delayed forever.

